                <pb facs="0811" n="717" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg717"/>

                <head>No. 458.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Manning.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-04-27"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 27,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 93.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 117, of the 15th
                    instant, relative to the Mexican law concerning foreigners.</p>
                <p>While appreciating the disposition of the Mexican Government to afford
                    opportunity to citizens of the United States who desire to do so to comply with
                    the provisions of the law in question of June Jast, this Department, for the
                    reasons set forth in its instructions of November 20 last, is still compelled to
                    dissent from the position that foreigners who have purchased land or had
                    children born to them in Mexico may, from time to time, by a municipal statute,
                    be deprived of their nationality unless they take some affirmative step to
                    preserve it.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
